---
import GroupCards from '../../../../../../../components/GroupCards.astro';
import Term from '../../../../../../../components/Term.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../../../../../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { sourceUrl } from '../../../../../../../data/aircraft-procedures';
import { groupPages } from '../../../../../../../data/manual-menu';
import { recordRoute, includedRecords } from '../../../../../../../data/encyclopedia';
import { sitePath } from '../../../../../../../lib/paths';

// The AIM-120 AMRAAM article: the parent of the AIM-120 procedures (`article: true` in the menu).
const node = groupPages().find(n => n.id === 'weapon-aim-120')!;
const record = includedRecords.find(r => r.slug === 'aim-120');
---
<SiteLayout title="AIM-120 AMRAAM" activeSection="manual" description="The F-16C's AIM-120 AMRAAM: how the active radar missile guides, its SLAVE and BORE launch modes, its HUD and radar symbols, and procedures for firing it.">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="lead">The AIM-120 AMRAAM is the F-16C's missile for shots beyond visual range. It is an active radar missile: it flies most of the way on the jet's radar data, then turns on a small radar of its own and finds the target itself, so the shooter can turn away before it arrives.{record && <> Its size, speed and history are in the <a href={sitePath(recordRoute(record))}>Encyclopedia</a>.</>} <a href="#source-1">[1, p. 590]</a></p>

    <h2>Procedures</h2>
    <GroupCards node={node} />

    <h2>How it guides</h2>
    <p>Before launch the jet passes the target's position, course and speed to the missile through its rail. In flight the missile steers by its own inertial system toward where the target will be; while the jet's radar keeps tracking the target, the jet sends it updates by datalink. Near the target its seeker goes active and it homes on its own. Against a jamming target it can home on the jamming instead. <a href="#source-1">[1, pp. 590–591]</a></p>

    <h2>Launch modes</h2>
    <p>The SMS page sets the missile's line of sight. In <strong>SLAVE</strong> it is sent after the radar's bugged target, the target of interest, in <Term id="Situation awareness mode">SAM</Term>, <Term id="Single target track">STT</Term>, TWS or the two-target modes; the jet can guide up to six missiles at separate targets. In <strong>BORE</strong>, or in SLAVE with nothing bugged, the seeker goes active as the missile leaves the rail and it takes the first aircraft it finds ahead, friend or foe. <a href="#source-1">[1, pp. 592, 600]</a></p>
    <p>The quickest way to the missile is the DOG FIGHT switch on the throttle, slid inboard: <Term>missile override</Term> selects the AIM-120 and sets up the radar without a hand leaving the controls. The ICP's A-A button does the same in the A-A master mode, with the SMS's AAM sub-mode. <a href="#source-1">[1, pp. 558–559]</a></p>

    <h2>Symbols</h2>
    <p>With an AIM-120 selected and a target bugged, the HUD and the radar page show the same shot cues. The <Term>ASEC</Term> circle and the <Term>ASC</Term>, a small circle, steer the shot: fly the ASC into the ASEC. The missile scale beside them shows the target's range and closure against the missile's reach: <Term>RAERO</Term>, <Term>ROPT</Term>, and the <Term>DLZ</Term> from <Term>RPI</Term> down, with <Term>RTR</Term> and <Term>RMIN</Term> bounding its box. Under it, a pole and a timer: the range when the missile would go active (<Term>M-pole</Term>) and the seconds until it does (A), then, once it is active, the range at impact (<Term>F-pole</Term>) and the seconds to it (T). With nothing bugged the HUD shows a missile reticle round a diamond, 6° below the boresight cross, for a boresight shot. <a href="#source-1">[1, pp. 593–598]</a></p>

    <section class="sources" aria-labelledby="aim120-sources"><h2 id="aim120-sources">Sources</h2><ol><li id="source-1"><a href={sourceUrl}>DCS F-16C Viper Early Access Guide (EN, 16 August 2026)</a> — Eagle Dynamics. Master modes pp. 558–559; “AIM-120 AMRAAM” pp. 590–607. A sim manual: it describes the DCS F-16C, which models the real aircraft's weapons in simplified form.</li></ol></section>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
